#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
courier - autonomous parcel collection and delivery for one drone.

Flies the aircraft to the box it has been assigned, descends onto it, welds it
on, carries it across the yard, sets it down on the destination depot and
climbs away. The flying is the reactive laser controller from uav_bushfire,
largely unchanged and for the same reasons; what is new is that the job has two
ends.

Algorithm
---------
  TAKEOFF - engage the motors and climb to cruise_altitude. Nothing else can
            happen until the aircraft is airborne.
  ALIGN   - yaw in place until the drone is facing the leg's goal.
  CHASE   - fly forward along the current heading at cruise altitude, with
            proportional yaw correction. Speed scales down inside slow_dist
            and again near obstacles.
  DESCEND - overhead the goal: stop flying forward, hold station over it and
            come down to the working altitude.
  ACT     - GRASP on the pickup leg, RELEASE on the delivery leg.
  CLIMB   - back to cruise altitude before going anywhere.
  IDLE    - airborne with no job, holding position.

One task, two legs
------------------
Everything above runs twice per parcel. A fire or a treasure is one place the
aircraft has to reach; a parcel is two, in order, and the second only becomes
reachable by doing something at the first. So the state machine carries a leg
alongside its phase - PICKUP, then DELIVERY - and ALIGN/CHASE/DESCEND/ACT are
the same code pointed at a different goal with a different action at the
bottom. The alternative, a phase per leg, doubles the machine to say the same
thing twice.

The two legs are not symmetrical in one respect, and it is the respect that
bites: on the pickup leg the drone is empty and the thing it is descending onto
is loose, so a sloppy approach knocks the box away; on the delivery leg it is
carrying 0.04 kg of unmodelled inertia, so the same approach is flown by a
slightly different aircraft than the one the gains were tuned for.

Why the descent is a separate phase
-----------------------------------
The pickup and delivery points are on the ground and cannot be flown to - the
aircraft would have to land on them. So arrival and action come apart: CHASE
ends *overhead* at cruise, and then the drone has to get down through the last
metre onto something it is directly above.

That descent cannot be flown body-forward the way CHASE is, because "forward"
is not a useful direction when the error is 3 cm on an arbitrary bearing and
the drone is not yawing any more. DESCEND rotates the world-frame position
error into the body frame and commands both linear.x and linear.y, which is
proper station-keeping. It is the one place this node uses lateral velocity,
and translating sideways is exactly what makes the laser's sectors meaningless,
which is why CHASE never does it.

The descent is also gated, which the bushfire version did not need to be. A
water drop is over when the aircraft is low enough; a grasp is over when the
pad is on the box, and the pad is 0.09 m across. So DESCEND only descends while
the horizontal error is inside grasp_tolerance and simply holds height when it
is not, rather than continuing down and missing. Sinking onto a box you are
5 cm off is how you knock it across the yard.

CLIMB is not decoration either. Leaving at working altitude means crossing the
yard at 0.31 m with a box slung underneath, below the height the geofence and
the avoidance were tuned for, and into whatever the next depot is next to.

Which frame /cmd_vel is in
--------------------------
The body one, yaw-stabilized. This is worth stating flatly because getting it
wrong is silent and fatal, and this package had it wrong: TwistController's
cmd_vel callback sets

    command_given_in_stabilized_frame_ = true;      // twist_controller.cpp:139

and update() then rotates the command by the current yaw before using it. Only
the command/twist topic - which nothing here publishes - is taken in the world
frame.

So a command of (speed, 0) means "forward", whatever the drone is pointed at.
Building the command as (speed*cos(yaw), speed*sin(yaw)) - which is what a
world-frame interface would want - gets rotated by yaw a second time and flies
the aircraft along 2*yaw. That is not a wobble: at yaw 95 degrees the drone
travels at 190, i.e. backwards and sideways, with its laser pointed at empty
air. Measured that exactly - a drone yawed to 94.7 degrees moving along 215 -
before this was found, and the whole fleet spent every run grinding into walls
it was not looking at and scoring nothing.

Everything this node publishes is therefore body-frame: linear.x is forward,
linear.y is unused, and linear.z is untouched by the transform and so is the
same in either frame.

Why ALIGN exists on an aircraft
-------------------------------
Given the above it is not optional, but the reason is the laser rather than the
frame. The scanner is bolted to the airframe, so its "front" sector only means
"where I am going" if the drone is pointed where it is going. ALIGN is what
keeps those two the same thing; CHASE then flies body-forward and the safety
filter's three sectors mean what their names say.

Staying out of each other's way
-------------------------------
Three aircraft sharing a yard need more than the laser, and the reason is
geometric rather than a matter of tuning. The scanner is a horizontal plane on
a mast: it sees another drone beautifully when the two are at the same height
and cannot see it at all when one is above or below. That is precisely the
geometry of the cases that matter here - one aircraft descending onto a depot
while another crosses over the top of it, or two climbing away from adjacent
depots into the same piece of sky.

So deconfliction is done twice, structurally and reactively.

Structurally, each courier transits on its own altitude layer: uav_0 at 1.3 m,
uav_1 at 1.9, uav_2 at 2.5. A drone occupies roughly 0.56 m vertically - mast
top at +0.25, a slung box at -0.31 - so a 0.6 m layer gap keeps transits from
overlapping at all, and most of a run is transit. The layers all sit under the
3 m crates, which is why those are as tall as the walls: a layer that cleared
the obstacle field would be a layer whose drone never uses its laser.

Reactively, and for everything the layers do not cover - the depots, where all
three layers collapse onto the same descent - the courier knows where the other
aircraft are, because it subscribes to their odometry. Inside separation_xy
horizontally and separation_z vertically, one of the two yields: it stops,
holds the altitude it had, and lets the other through.

Which one yields has to be decided the same way by both, or they either both
stop forever or neither does. The rule is: a loaded aircraft has right of way
over an empty one, and between two in the same state the lower namespace wins.
Both facts are known to both parties - who is carrying comes from the
dispatcher's parcel board, which every courier already receives - so the two
always reach opposite conclusions, and there is no negotiation to go wrong.

Geofence and rescue
-------------------
A drone that is upset - clipped a crate, caught another drone's downwash - does
not recover on its own here, and the reason is worth writing down. The launch
sets hector's auto_engage false, and in this scenario that is not optional at
all: auto_engage cuts the motors when it sees a commanded descent that is not
being achieved, which is landing detection, and DESCEND is a commanded descent
that ends 0.31 m off the deck and holds there. But twist_controller.cpp nests
its flip-over check *inside* the same auto_engage block:

    if (auto_engage_) {
      ...
      if (motors_running_ && load_factor < 0.0) {   // flip over?
        motors_running_ = false;

so turning the one off turns the other off with it. An inverted aircraft then
keeps full thrust pointing wherever its rotors happen to face and leaves. That
is not a hypothetical: uav_1 clipped a wall, went over it at 3.5 m, and was
still accelerating 275 m away with the motors running when the run ended.

So there are two fences. Outside fence_half_size the drone stops working the
parcel and flies back to the middle - an ordinary soft geofence. Past
fence_hard_size, or tilted past upset_angle, it is no longer a control problem,
and the drone is teleported back to its launch point with
/gazebo/set_model_state and re-engaged. Respawning is the honest fix in a
simulator: there is no attitude recovery to write, because the controller that
would fly it is the one that has already lost the aircraft.

A respawn has to cut the motors on the way past, not just move the aircraft.
TwistController only clears its PID integrators in the branch it takes when the
motors are *not* running, so with auto_engage off there is no path that ever
resets them. Teleporting alone leaves the attitude loop wound up against the
tumble it was fighting a moment ago, and it spends that integral on the
freshly-levelled aircraft and puts it straight back over. That is a respawn
loop, and it is self-sustaining: 53 rescues in 90 seconds, none of which flew.
So the order is /shutdown, teleport, settle, /engage - the shutdown is what
makes the reset happen. Rescues are also rate-limited by settle_time, because
a drone that has just been dropped in bounces on its landing gear and the
attitude that produces is not an upset.

What the laser cannot see
-------------------------
It is a horizontal 270-degree plane. It says nothing about what is above or
below the aircraft, so altitude is flown open-loop with respect to obstacles
and only min_altitude keeps it off the ground. That is survivable here because
the crates and the walls are vertical and run the full height the drone flies
at, so anything it could hit in transit, it can also see. A scene with real
overhangs would need a sensor this aircraft does not have.

It also means the descent is blind downwards, and this package leans on that
harder than its siblings did, because the descent ends 0.31 m off the deck with
a box slung below. Nothing stops the drone flying into the ground except
work_altitude and min_altitude being honest numbers - and min_altitude here has
to be *lower* than the working altitude rather than a floor above it, which is
why it is a separate parameter and not simply the same number.

Self-hits, and why the filter for them is conditional
-----------------------------------------------------
Past about 32 degrees of tilt the scan plane dips through the rotor discs and
the drone sees its own booms at their tip radius, ~0.40 m (the mast height in
urdf/quadrotor_courier.gazebo.xacro is where that number comes from). Read
as obstacles those returns mean "boxed in", and the aircraft stops for good.

The obvious guard - drop everything closer than 0.55 m - is worse than the
disease, because it is precisely the range at which this drone gets into
trouble. Jammed against a crate the laser reads 0.33 m; wedged in a corner,
0.24 m. Both are below the threshold, so both are discarded, and a drone in
contact with a wall sees a clear path through it and holds full forward thrust
against it until it tips over. Two of the three drones ended a run that way.

So the rejection is conditional on the thing that causes it. Tilt is known -
it is in the same odometry the controller already reads - and below
self_hit_tilt the plane is provably above the rotors, so returns are trusted
down to the sensor floor. Above it, returns inside self_hit_range are dropped.
The drone is briefly blind while recovering hard, which is the one moment it is
not steering by the laser anyway.

Subscriptions
-------------
  ground_truth/state      nav_msgs/Odometry      Drone pose + velocity
  /<other>/ground_truth/state  nav_msgs/Odometry Every other aircraft
  /delivery/parcels       uav_delivery/ParcelArray  Who is carrying what
  delivery/task           uav_delivery/Parcel    This drone's job
  /gazebo/model_states    gazebo_msgs/ModelStates Where the box really is
  scan                    sensor_msgs/LaserScan  Hokuyo, on the mast

Publications
------------
  cmd_vel                 geometry_msgs/Twist    Body-frame velocity (~20 Hz)
  /delivery/report        uav_delivery/Parcel    Grasped / released / gave up

Services used
-------------
  engage                  std_srvs/Empty         Spin the motors up
  shutdown                std_srvs/Empty         Cut them, to clear the PIDs
  /link_attacher_node/attach   gazebo_ros_link_attacher/Attach   the gripper
  /link_attacher_node/detach   gazebo_ros_link_attacher/Attach

ROS Parameters (private, ~)
---------------------------
  ~cruise_altitude float  Transit altitude, m                (default 1.4)
  ~grasp_reach     float  Pad below base_link; match the urdf(default 0.21)
  ~box_size        float  Edge of a box, m                   (default 0.10)
  ~min_altitude    float  Never command below this, m        (default 0.26)
  ~grasp_tolerance float  Descend only when centred within
                          this, m                            (default 0.060)
  ~hover_margin    float  Hold this far above the working
                          altitude while centring, m         (default 0.20)
  ~grasp_z_tol     float  Working altitude reached within, m (default 0.030)
  ~act_timeout     float  Give the parcel back after this
                          long stuck in a descent, s         (default 40.0)
  ~linear_speed    float  Max forward speed (m/s)            (default 1.2)
  ~vertical_speed  float  Max climb/descent rate (m/s)       (default 0.6)
  ~station_gain    float  P-gain for holding over the goal   (default 1.2)
  ~station_speed   float  Speed cap while station-keeping    (default 0.6)
  ~angular_gain    float  P-gain for heading error           (default 2.0)
  ~max_angular     float  Yaw rate cap (rad/s)               (default 1.5)
  ~angle_tol       float  Heading tolerance (rad)            (default 0.12)
  ~slow_dist       float  Begin slowing at this dist (m)     (default 1.0)
  ~arrive_radius   float  Switch to DESCEND inside this, m   (default 0.60)
  ~fleet           list   Every drone namespace, for traffic (default [])
  ~separation_xy   float  Traffic conflict radius, m         (default 1.5)
  ~separation_z    float  ...and vertically, m               (default 0.5)
  ~yield_speed     float  Speed to back away when giving way (default 0.5)
  ~depot_guard_xy  float  Do not descend if another aircraft
                          is this close to my goal, m        (default 1.3)
  ~depot_guard_z   float  ...and below work_alt plus this, m (default 0.7)
  ~climb_tol       float  Cruise reached within this, m      (default 0.20)
  ~avoid_dist      float  Avoidance activation range (m)     (default 1.6)
  ~stop_dist       float  Full-stop obstacle range (m)       (default 0.9)
  ~min_valid_range float  Sensor floor; drop returns below   (default 0.15)
  ~self_hit_range  float  Drop returns below this when tilted(default 0.50)
  ~self_hit_tilt   float  Tilt at which the mast stops
                          clearing the rotors, rad           (default 0.50)
  ~back_off_speed  float  Reverse speed when fully blocked   (default 0.35)
  ~avoid_hold      float  Stay committed to a deflection for
                          this long after it clears, s       (default 1.5)
  ~fence_half_size float  Soft geofence half-width, m        (default 4.8)
  ~fence_hard_size float  Respawn beyond this half-width, m  (default 8.0)
  ~fence_max_alt   float  Respawn above this altitude, m     (default 4.0)
  ~upset_angle     float  Respawn past this roll/pitch, rad  (default 1.05)
  ~settle_time     float  Ignore upsets for this long after
                          a respawn, s                       (default 1.5)
  ~start_x         float  Launch point, for respawns         (default 0.0)
  ~start_y         float  Launch point, for respawns         (default 0.0)
  ~control_hz      float  Control loop rate (Hz)             (default 20.0)
  ~status_hz       float  Status display rate (Hz)           (default 2.0)
"""

import math
import sys

import rospy

from geometry_msgs.msg import Twist
from nav_msgs.msg import Odometry
from sensor_msgs.msg import LaserScan
from std_srvs.srv import Empty
from gazebo_msgs.msg import ModelState, ModelStates
from gazebo_msgs.srv import SetModelState
from gazebo_ros_link_attacher.srv import Attach, AttachRequest
from tf.transformations import euler_from_quaternion

from uav_delivery.msg import Parcel, ParcelArray

# See the note in scripts/dispatcher: roslaunch pipes a node's output and
# Python block-buffers into a pipe. rospy puts WARN and ERROR on stderr, which
# on Python 3.8 buffers the same way, so without this the warnings that matter
# most - rescues, given-up parcels - arrive late or not at all.
sys.stdout.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)
sys.stderr.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)

_RESET  = '\033[0m'
_BOLD   = '\033[1m'
_YELLOW = '\033[93m'
_GREEN  = '\033[92m'
_CYAN   = '\033[96m'
_RED    = '\033[91m'
_WHITE  = '\033[97m'


def _bar(value, max_value=10.0, width=10):
    filled = round(width * value / max(max_value, 1))
    return _GREEN + '#' * filled + _RESET + '-' * (width - filled)


def _normalize_angle(a):
    while a > math.pi:
        a -= 2.0 * math.pi
    while a < -math.pi:
        a += 2.0 * math.pi
    return a


def _clamp(v, lo, hi):
    return max(lo, min(hi, v))


class Courier:

    def __init__(self):
        rospy.init_node('courier', anonymous=False)

        self._ns = rospy.get_namespace().strip('/') or 'uav'

        self._cruise_alt   = rospy.get_param('~cruise_altitude', 1.4)
        self._grasp_reach  = rospy.get_param('~grasp_reach',     0.21)
        self._box_size     = rospy.get_param('~box_size',        0.10)
        self._min_alt      = rospy.get_param('~min_altitude',    0.26)
        self._grasp_tol    = rospy.get_param('~grasp_tolerance', 0.060)
        self._hover_margin = rospy.get_param('~hover_margin',    0.20)
        self._grasp_z_tol  = rospy.get_param('~grasp_z_tol',     0.030)
        self._act_timeout  = rospy.get_param('~act_timeout',     40.0)

        # The one altitude the whole job turns on. To put the pad on top of a
        # box sitting on the ground, base_link has to be at box_size (the top
        # face) plus grasp_reach (the pad's offset below base_link). The same
        # number does for the delivery leg, because setting a carried box down
        # on the ground means putting its underside at zero, which is the pose
        # it was welded in.
        self._work_alt = self._box_size + self._grasp_reach
        self._lin_speed    = rospy.get_param('~linear_speed',    1.2)
        self._vert_speed   = rospy.get_param('~vertical_speed',  0.6)
        self._stn_gain     = rospy.get_param('~station_gain',    1.2)
        self._stn_speed    = rospy.get_param('~station_speed',   0.6)
        self._ang_gain     = rospy.get_param('~angular_gain',    2.0)
        self._max_ang      = rospy.get_param('~max_angular',     1.5)
        self._angle_tol    = rospy.get_param('~angle_tol',       0.12)
        self._slow_dist    = rospy.get_param('~slow_dist',       1.0)
        self._arrive_rad   = rospy.get_param('~arrive_radius',   0.60)
        self._fleet        = rospy.get_param('~fleet',           [])
        self._sep_xy       = rospy.get_param('~separation_xy',   1.5)
        self._sep_z        = rospy.get_param('~separation_z',    0.5)
        self._yield_speed  = rospy.get_param('~yield_speed',      0.5)
        self._depot_guard_xy = rospy.get_param('~depot_guard_xy', 1.3)
        self._depot_guard_z  = rospy.get_param('~depot_guard_z',  0.7)
        self._climb_tol    = rospy.get_param('~climb_tol',       0.20)
        self._avoid_dist   = rospy.get_param('~avoid_dist',      1.6)
        self._stop_dist    = rospy.get_param('~stop_dist',       0.9)
        self._min_range    = rospy.get_param('~min_valid_range', 0.15)
        self._self_hit     = rospy.get_param('~self_hit_range',  0.50)
        self._self_hit_tilt = rospy.get_param('~self_hit_tilt',  0.50)
        self._back_off     = rospy.get_param('~back_off_speed',  0.35)
        self._avoid_hold   = rospy.get_param('~avoid_hold',      1.5)
        self._fence        = rospy.get_param('~fence_half_size', 4.8)
        self._fence_hard   = rospy.get_param('~fence_hard_size', 8.0)
        self._fence_alt    = rospy.get_param('~fence_max_alt',   4.0)
        self._upset_angle  = rospy.get_param('~upset_angle',     1.05)
        self._settle_time  = rospy.get_param('~settle_time',     1.5)
        self._start_x      = rospy.get_param('~start_x',         0.0)
        self._start_y      = rospy.get_param('~start_y',         0.0)
        control_hz         = rospy.get_param('~control_hz',     20.0)
        status_hz          = rospy.get_param('~status_hz',       2.0)

        self._x = self._y = self._z = 0.0
        self._roll = self._pitch = self._yaw = 0.0
        self._odom_ready = False
        self._rescues = 0
        self._settle_until = rospy.Time(0)

        self._scan = None
        self._avoiding = False
        self._avoid_until = rospy.Time(0)
        self._fenced = False

        self._parcel = None          # the job, as the dispatcher stated it
        self._holding = None         # box model name, or None
        self._awaiting = False       # released; waiting for the dispatcher\'s verdict
        self._state = 'TAKEOFF'
        self._wait_ticks = 0
        self._descend_since = None   # when this descent started, for the timeout
        self._delivered = 0
        self._history = []

        # Where the box actually is, rather than where the depot is. On the
        # pickup leg these differ: the dispatcher puts a box down at a depot,
        # but a box that was previously misdelivered sits wherever it landed,
        # and descending onto the depot marking instead of onto the box would
        # grasp thin air.
        self._box_pose = {}

        # Traffic. Positions of every other aircraft, and which of them are
        # loaded - the second half comes free off the dispatcher's board, so
        # right-of-way needs no extra topic and no negotiation.
        self._others = {}            # ns -> (x, y, z)
        self._loaded = set()         # namespaces currently carrying a box
        self._yielding = False
        self._yield_z = None
        self._yield_began = None
        for other in self._fleet:
            if other == self._ns:
                continue
            rospy.Subscriber('/%s/ground_truth/state' % other, Odometry,
                             self._other_cb, callback_args=other)
        rospy.Subscriber('/delivery/parcels', ParcelArray, self._board_cb)

        rospy.Subscriber('ground_truth/state', Odometry, self._odom_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('delivery/task', Parcel, self._task_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('scan', LaserScan, self._scan_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('/gazebo/model_states', ModelStates, self._models_cb,
                         queue_size=1)

        self._cmd_pub = rospy.Publisher('cmd_vel', Twist, queue_size=1)
        self._report_pub = rospy.Publisher('/delivery/report', Parcel,
                                           queue_size=10)

        # Held open rather than looked up per call: a rescue is the one moment
        # this node is in a hurry, and it needs all three of these.
        self._engage_srv = None
        self._shutdown_srv = None
        self._set_state_srv = None
        self._attach_srv = None
        self._detach_srv = None
        self._connect_services()

        self._engage()

        rospy.Timer(rospy.Duration(1.0 / control_hz), self._control_loop)
        rospy.Timer(rospy.Duration(1.0 / status_hz), self._status_display)

        self._print_header()

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ motors ---
    def _connect_services(self):
        try:
            rospy.wait_for_service('engage', timeout=60.0)
            rospy.wait_for_service('shutdown', timeout=10.0)
            rospy.wait_for_service('/gazebo/set_model_state', timeout=10.0)
        except rospy.ROSException as exc:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] services never appeared: %s', self._ns, exc)
            return
        self._engage_srv    = rospy.ServiceProxy('engage', Empty)
        self._shutdown_srv  = rospy.ServiceProxy('shutdown', Empty)
        self._set_state_srv = rospy.ServiceProxy('/gazebo/set_model_state',
                                                 SetModelState)

        # The gripper. If the world plugin did not load these never appear, and
        # the failure is worth naming precisely, because it is almost always
        # GAZEBO_PLUGIN_PATH rather than anything in this package.
        try:
            rospy.wait_for_service('/link_attacher_node/attach', timeout=30.0)
        except rospy.ROSException:
            rospy.logfatal(
                '[%s] /link_attacher_node/attach never appeared. '
                'gazebo_ros_link_attacher did not load - check that '
                'GAZEBO_PLUGIN_PATH contains ~/uav_ws/devel/lib (source '
                'setup_uav.bash) and that depot_yard.world still has the '
                'ros_link_attacher_plugin <plugin> tag.', self._ns)
            return
        self._attach_srv = rospy.ServiceProxy('/link_attacher_node/attach', Attach)
        self._detach_srv = rospy.ServiceProxy('/link_attacher_node/detach', Attach)

    def _engage(self):
        """Spin the motors up.

        The launch turns hector's auto_engage off. Left on it engages on the
        first commanded climb and, more to the point, cuts the motors when it
        sees a commanded descent it cannot achieve. That is landing detection,
        and DESCEND is indistinguishable from a landing by design: a commanded
        descent that ends 0.31 m off the deck and holds there while a weld is
        made or broken.
        """
        if self._engage_srv is None:
            return
        try:
            self._engage_srv()
            rospy.loginfo('[%s] motors engaged', self._ns)
        except rospy.ServiceException as exc:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] could not engage motors: %s', self._ns, exc)

    def _shutdown_motors(self):
        """Cut the motors, which is the only thing that resets the PIDs.

        See "Geofence and rescue" above: with auto_engage off, TwistController
        never takes the branch that calls reset(), so this is the sole way to
        empty its integrators.
        """
        if self._shutdown_srv is None:
            return
        try:
            self._shutdown_srv()
        except rospy.ServiceException as exc:
            rospy.logwarn('[%s] could not shut motors down: %s', self._ns, exc)

    # ------------------------------------------------------------- fence ---
    def _lost(self):
        """True if the aircraft is beyond saving by ordinary control."""
        return (abs(self._x) > self._fence_hard or
                abs(self._y) > self._fence_hard or
                self._z > self._fence_alt or
                abs(self._roll) > self._upset_angle or
                abs(self._pitch) > self._upset_angle)

    def _outside_fence(self):
        return abs(self._x) > self._fence or abs(self._y) > self._fence

    def _rescue(self):
        """Teleport back to the launch point, stopped and level, and re-engage.

        Order matters. The motors go off *first*: that is what empties the
        attitude PIDs, and a teleport without it hands the freshly-levelled
        aircraft the integral the controller had wound up fighting the tumble.
        """
        # Let go first. A box is welded to base_link with a fixed joint, and
        # teleporting one end of a fixed joint across the yard is not a state
        # Gazebo has any reason to handle gracefully. Dropping it also gets the
        # parcel back on the board: it falls, comes to rest, and the dispatcher
        # re-lists it from wherever that turns out to be.
        if self._holding is not None:
            rospy.logwarn('[%s] dropping %s to be rescued', self._ns,
                          self._holding)
            self._release()
            self._report(Parcel.WAITING)
            self._parcel = None
            self._awaiting = False
            self._descend_since = None

        self._rescues += 1
        rospy.logwarn('[%s] RESCUE #%d - lost at (%.1f, %.1f, %.1f) '
                      'roll=%.0f pitch=%.0f deg; respawning at (%.1f, %.1f)',
                      self._ns, self._rescues, self._x, self._y, self._z,
                      math.degrees(self._roll), math.degrees(self._pitch),
                      self._start_x, self._start_y)

        self._cmd_pub.publish(Twist())
        self._shutdown_motors()

        state = ModelState()
        state.model_name = self._ns
        state.reference_frame = 'world'
        state.pose.position.x = self._start_x
        state.pose.position.y = self._start_y
        state.pose.position.z = 0.3
        state.pose.orientation.w = 1.0
        if self._set_state_srv is None:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] respawn unavailable - no set_model_state',
                         self._ns)
            return
        try:
            self._set_state_srv(state)
        except rospy.ServiceException as exc:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] respawn failed: %s', self._ns, exc)
            return

        # Let the teleport settle before commanding anything, or the first
        # command is computed against the pose it had before it moved. The
        # motors are off through this, so nothing is being flown.
        rospy.sleep(0.5)
        self._cmd_pub.publish(Twist())
        self._engage()
        self._state = 'TAKEOFF'
        self._avoiding = False
        self._yielding = False
        self._yield_z = None

        # Landing gear bounce is not an upset. Without this hold the next tick
        # sees the attitude of a drone still settling on its feet and rescues
        # it again, which is how a single bad moment became 53 of them.
        self._settle_until = rospy.Time.now() + rospy.Duration(self._settle_time)

    # ------------------------------------------------------ laser helpers ---
    def _sector_min(self, deg_from, deg_to):
        """Minimum valid range in an angular sector, degrees, 0 = forward.

        The scan is 270 degrees centred on forward, not the TurtleBot3's full
        360, so index-to-angle uses angle_min and angle_increment from the
        message rather than tb3_treasure's `i * 360 / n`. That shortcut is only
        correct for a scanner that closes the circle, and using it here would
        silently rotate every sector by 45 degrees.

        The floor is normally just the sensor's own, so a wall the drone is
        already touching is still a wall. It rises to self_hit_range only while
        the aircraft is tilted past the angle at which the mast stops clearing
        the rotors - see "Self-hits" at the top of this file.
        """
        scan = self._scan
        if scan is None:
            return float('inf')

        floor = max(scan.range_min, self._min_range)
        if max(abs(self._roll), abs(self._pitch)) > self._self_hit_tilt:
            floor = max(floor, self._self_hit)
        a = deg_from % 360.0
        b = deg_to % 360.0
        best = float('inf')
        for i, r in enumerate(scan.ranges):
            if not math.isfinite(r) or not (floor < r < scan.range_max):
                continue
            deg = math.degrees(scan.angle_min + i * scan.angle_increment) % 360.0
            in_sector = (a <= deg <= b) if a <= b else (deg >= a or deg <= b)
            if in_sector and r < best:
                best = r
        return best

    def _safety_filter(self, cmd):
        """Reactive obstacle avoidance, applied to CHASE commands.

        Same three-sector logic as tb3_treasure - front +-45, left 10-80,
        right 280-350 - and, like tb3_treasure's, it works directly on
        cmd.linear.x, because /cmd_vel here is body-frame too. Vertical
        velocity is left alone: the laser has nothing to say about it.
        """
        front = self._sector_min(315.0, 45.0)

        if front > self._avoid_dist:
            self._avoiding = False
            return cmd

        self._avoiding = True
        self._avoid_until = rospy.Time.now() + rospy.Duration(self._avoid_hold)
        left = self._sector_min(10.0, 80.0)
        right = self._sector_min(280.0, 350.0)
        turn_left = left >= right

        if front <= self._stop_dist:
            # Stop, back away, and yaw toward the clearer side.
            #
            # tb3_treasure only stops and spins, which works for a robot that
            # can pivot on the spot and then drive out along a new heading.
            # This one cannot always: caught against a wall it spins, and every
            # heading it turns to still has the wall in front, so it spins for
            # the rest of the run. Observed exactly that - all three drones
            # pinned to walls at max yaw rate, scoring nothing. Reversing is
            # what actually breaks the deadlock, and it is free here because a
            # multirotor has no preferred direction of travel.
            cmd.linear.x = -self._back_off
            cmd.linear.y = 0.0
            cmd.angular.z = self._max_ang if turn_left else -self._max_ang
        else:
            ratio = ((front - self._stop_dist) /
                     (self._avoid_dist - self._stop_dist))
            cmd.linear.x *= ratio

            avoid_w = self._max_ang * (1.0 - ratio)
            if turn_left:
                cmd.angular.z = min(self._max_ang, cmd.angular.z + avoid_w)
            else:
                cmd.angular.z = max(-self._max_ang, cmd.angular.z - avoid_w)

        return cmd

    # ----------------------------------------------------------- display ---
    def _print_header(self):
        print()
        print(_BOLD + _WHITE + '+--------------------------------------------------+' + _RESET)
        print(_BOLD + _WHITE + '|   UAV COURIER  -  %-30s |' % self._ns + _RESET)
        print(_BOLD + _WHITE + '+--------------------------------------------------+' + _RESET)
        print('  %scruise_alt%s   = %.2f m     %swork_altitude%s = %.2f m'
              % (_CYAN, _RESET, self._cruise_alt, _CYAN, _RESET, self._work_alt))
        print('  %sarrive_rad%s   = %.2f m     %sgrasp_tol%s     = %.3f m'
              % (_CYAN, _RESET, self._arrive_rad, _CYAN, _RESET, self._grasp_tol))
        print('  %savoid_dist%s   = %.2f m     %sstop_dist%s     = %.2f m'
              % (_CYAN, _RESET, self._avoid_dist, _CYAN, _RESET, self._stop_dist))
        print()

    def _print_delivery(self, n, parcel):
        print(_BOLD + _GREEN + '+--------------------------------------------------+' + _RESET)
        print(_BOLD + _GREEN + '|  *** DELIVERED  %-6s  (parcel #%-3d) ***       |'
              % (self._ns, parcel.id) + _RESET)
        print(_BOLD + _GREEN + '+--------------------------------------------------+' + _RESET)
        print('  Route            : %s%s%s  ->  %s%s%s'
              % (_YELLOW, parcel.src_name, _RESET, _YELLOW, parcel.dst_name, _RESET))
        print('  Set down at      : (%6.2f, %6.2f) m' % (parcel.dst_x, parcel.dst_y))
        print('  Released from    : (%6.2f, %6.2f, %5.2f) m'
              % (self._x, self._y, self._z))
        print('  Delivered so far : %s%s%4d%s' % (_YELLOW, _BOLD, n, _RESET))
        print()
        if len(self._history) > 1:
            print('  Recent runs:')
            for i, route in self._history[-5:]:
                marker = _GREEN + '>>>' + _RESET if i == n else '   '
                print('    %s #%-3d  %s' % (marker, i, route))
            print()

    def _status_display(self, _event):
        if not self._odom_ready:
            return
        state_col = {'ALIGN': _CYAN, 'CHASE': _GREEN, 'DESCEND': _YELLOW,
                     'CLIMB': _CYAN, 'ACT': _GREEN, 'WAIT': _YELLOW,
                     'HOLD': _YELLOW, 'IDLE': _WHITE,
                     'TAKEOFF': _WHITE}.get(self._state, _WHITE)
        avoid_tag = ' %s[AVOID]%s' % (_RED, _RESET) if self._avoiding else ''
        if self._yielding:
            avoid_tag += ' %s[YIELD]%s' % (_RED, _RESET)
        # The fence replaces the goal, so say so - otherwise the status line
        # shows a depot the drone has temporarily stopped flying toward.
        if self._fenced:
            avoid_tag += ' %s[FENCE]%s' % (_RED, _RESET)
        load = (' %s[%s]%s' % (_YELLOW, self._holding, _RESET)
                if self._holding else '')

        if self._parcel is None:
            print('  [%s] [%s%-7s%s]%s pos=(%6.2f,%6.2f,%5.2f) no job | '
                  'delivered=%d'
                  % (self._ns, state_col, self._state, _RESET, avoid_tag,
                     self._x, self._y, self._z, self._delivered))
            return

        gx, gy = self._goal()
        dist_xy = math.hypot(gx - self._x, gy - self._y)
        p = self._parcel
        print('  [%s] [%s%-7s%s]%s%s pos=(%6.2f,%6.2f,%5.2f) #%d %s>%s '
              '%s-leg goal=(%5.2f,%5.2f) dist=%s%5.2f%s delivered=%s%s%3d%s'
              % (self._ns, state_col, self._state, _RESET, avoid_tag, load,
                 self._x, self._y, self._z, p.id, p.src_name, p.dst_name,
                 self._leg[:4].lower(), gx, gy,
                 _CYAN, dist_xy, _RESET, _YELLOW, _BOLD, self._delivered,
                 _RESET))

    # ------------------------------------------------------------- gripper --
    def _report(self, state):
        """Tell the dispatcher what just happened to the parcel."""
        if self._parcel is None:
            return
        msg = Parcel()
        msg.id = self._parcel.id
        msg.box = self._parcel.box
        msg.carrier = self._ns
        msg.state = state
        self._report_pub.publish(msg)

    def _grasp(self):
        """Weld the box to the airframe, wherever it currently sits.

        The joint is made at the two links\' present relative pose, so the box
        is not snapped into the gripper - it stays exactly where it is and the
        aircraft has to have positioned itself properly first. A sloppy
        approach gives a crooked carry, not a corrected one, which is the whole
        reason DESCEND refuses to come down while off-centre.

        The weld is to base_link rather than gripper_link because Gazebo folds
        fixed-joint children into their parent, so there is no body called
        gripper_link in the simulator for the attacher to find.
        """
        if self._attach_srv is None or self._parcel is None:
            return False
        req = AttachRequest()
        req.model_name_1 = self._ns
        req.link_name_1 = 'base_link'
        req.model_name_2 = self._parcel.box
        req.link_name_2 = 'link'
        try:
            self._attach_srv(req)
        except rospy.ServiceException as exc:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] attach failed: %s', self._ns, exc)
            return False
        self._holding = self._parcel.box
        rospy.loginfo('[%s] picked up %s at %s', self._ns, self._parcel.box,
                      self._parcel.src_name)
        self._report(Parcel.CARRIED)
        return True

    def _release(self):
        """Unweld the carried box and leave it where it is."""
        if self._detach_srv is None or self._holding is None:
            return
        req = AttachRequest()
        req.model_name_1 = self._ns
        req.link_name_1 = 'base_link'
        req.model_name_2 = self._holding
        req.link_name_2 = 'link'
        try:
            self._detach_srv(req)
        except rospy.ServiceException as exc:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] detach failed: %s', self._ns, exc)
            return
        rospy.loginfo('[%s] released %s at %s', self._ns, self._holding,
                      self._parcel.dst_name if self._parcel else '?')
        self._holding = None

    def _abandon(self, why):
        """Give the parcel back to the dispatcher.

        Called when a descent will not converge, and on a respawn. Somebody
        has to say so out loud: the dispatcher\'s assignments are sticky, so a
        parcel whose courier quietly stopped working it would sit claimed
        forever and no other aircraft would ever be offered it.
        """
        rospy.logwarn('[%s] giving up parcel %s: %s', self._ns,
                      self._parcel.id if self._parcel else '?', why)
        self._release()
        self._report(Parcel.WAITING)
        self._parcel = None
        self._awaiting = False
        self._descend_since = None
        self._state = 'CLIMB'

    # -------------------------------------------------------------- traffic --
    def _gives_way_to(self, other):
        """Do I yield to `other`? Both sides must answer this oppositely.

        A loaded aircraft has right of way over an empty one, because it is the
        one whose loss costs a parcel and because it is the less manoeuvrable
        of the two. Between two in the same state the lower namespace wins,
        which is arbitrary but total - and total is the property that matters,
        since a rule where both could yield is a rule where both stop forever.

        Both inputs are shared knowledge: positions come from odometry and the
        loaded set comes from the dispatcher's board, which every courier
        receives. Neither side is asking the other anything, so there is no
        negotiation to fail.
        """
        me_loaded = self._holding is not None
        other_loaded = other in self._loaded
        if me_loaded != other_loaded:
            return not me_loaded
        return self._ns > other

    def _traffic_conflict(self):
        """The nearest aircraft I must give way to, or None.

        This is the half of collision avoidance the laser cannot do. A
        horizontal scan plane sees another drone only when the two are at
        much the same height, and the encounters that matter here are exactly
        the ones where they are not: somebody descending onto a depot while
        somebody else crosses above it.
        """
        nearest, nearest_d = None, float('inf')
        for other, (ox, oy, oz) in self._others.items():
            if abs(oz - self._z) > self._sep_z:
                continue
            d = math.hypot(ox - self._x, oy - self._y)
            if d > self._sep_xy:
                continue
            if not self._gives_way_to(other):
                continue
            if d < nearest_d:
                nearest, nearest_d = other, d
        return nearest, nearest_d

    def _give_way(self, cmd, other):
        """Back away from `other`, holding the altitude we had.

        Backing away rather than merely stopping, and that distinction is the
        whole of it. The first version stopped dead, which sounds like the
        polite thing and does not work: the aircraft with right of way is still
        flying, and if its goal lies beyond the one that stopped, it flies
        straight into it. Measured - uav_1 gave way at 0.32 m and the pair
        closed to 0.08 m anyway, because only one of them was doing anything.
        Retreating along the line between the two makes the gap open regardless
        of what the other aircraft chooses to do, which is the property a
        collision avoidance rule actually needs.

        The retreat is a world-frame direction rotated into the body frame,
        like the descent, because /cmd_vel is body-stabilized.

        Altitude is held as a commanded *altitude*, not a commanded zero rate:
        controller/twist closes on velocity, so zero is "stop climbing" and the
        aircraft sinks about a centimetre a second underneath it.
        """
        if self._yield_z is None:
            self._yield_z = max(self._z, self._min_alt)

        ox, oy, _ = self._others[other]
        away_x, away_y = self._x - ox, self._y - oy
        norm = math.hypot(away_x, away_y)
        if norm < 1e-3:
            # Directly on top of each other in plan view: any direction will
            # do, so pick one deterministically rather than dividing by zero.
            away_x, away_y, norm = 1.0, 0.0, 1.0
        away_x, away_y = away_x / norm, away_y / norm

        bx = away_x * math.cos(self._yaw) + away_y * math.sin(self._yaw)
        by = -away_x * math.sin(self._yaw) + away_y * math.cos(self._yaw)
        cmd.linear.x = self._yield_speed * bx
        cmd.linear.y = self._yield_speed * by
        cmd.angular.z = 0.0
        cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (self._yield_z - self._z),
                              -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
        return cmd

    def _descent_clear(self):
        """Is the whole column below me, over my goal, free of other aircraft?

        The altitude layers separate transits completely, and then every one of
        them collapses onto the same descent at a depot - which is exactly
        where the conflicts turned out to be. So the descent is serialised:
        while anything is in the way, I stay up on my own layer, where I am
        separated from everybody by construction, and queue.

        "In the way" means *anywhere below me* near the goal, not merely down
        in the depot, and the difference is not academic. The first version
        only looked for aircraft already at working height, so a drone on the
        top layer would happily start down through the middle layer at a point
        where another aircraft was transiting. Measured: uav_2 descending from
        2.6 m and uav_1 cruising at 1.9 passed within 0.09 m of each other, and
        neither noticed until they were already co-altitude - by which time the
        horizontal overlap was total and the yield rule had nothing useful left
        to do. A descent is a move through every layer underneath you, so the
        check has to cover every layer underneath you.

        Waiting up here costs a few seconds. Descending into traffic costs both
        aircraft.
        """
        gx, gy = self._goal()
        for ox, oy, oz in self._others.values():
            if oz > self._z - 0.20:
                continue          # at or above me, so not in the column below
            if math.hypot(ox - gx, oy - gy) < self._depot_guard_xy:
                return False
        return True

    # --------------------------------------------------------------- goals --
    @property
    def _leg(self):
        """Which half of the job this is - derived, never stored.

        A courier is on the delivery leg exactly when it is holding something.
        Keeping that as a separate flag invites the two to disagree, and there
        is a specific way it happens: a parcel that is put down off-target is
        re-listed by the dispatcher as a fresh pickup, and a stored leg would
        still say DELIVERY, sending an empty aircraft to the address to release
        a box it is not carrying.
        """
        return 'DELIVERY' if self._holding is not None else 'PICKUP'

    def _goal(self):
        """Where this leg is trying to get to, horizontally.

        On the pickup leg that is the box itself if Gazebo has told us where it
        is, and the stated source only as a fallback. Those differ whenever a
        box has been misdelivered and re-listed from where it came to rest, and
        descending onto a stale depot marking instead of onto the box is a
        grasp of thin air.
        """
        p = self._parcel
        if self._leg == 'DELIVERY':
            return p.dst_x, p.dst_y
        pos = self._box_pose.get(p.box)
        if pos is not None:
            return pos[0], pos[1]
        return p.src_x, p.src_y

    # --------------------------------------------------------- callbacks ---
    def _odom_cb(self, msg):
        self._x = msg.pose.pose.position.x
        self._y = msg.pose.pose.position.y
        self._z = msg.pose.pose.position.z
        q = msg.pose.pose.orientation
        self._roll, self._pitch, self._yaw = euler_from_quaternion(
            [q.x, q.y, q.z, q.w])
        self._odom_ready = True

    def _scan_cb(self, msg):
        self._scan = msg

    def _other_cb(self, msg, ns):
        p = msg.pose.pose.position
        self._others[ns] = (p.x, p.y, p.z)

    def _board_cb(self, msg):
        self._loaded = set(p.carrier for p in msg.parcels
                           if p.state == Parcel.CARRIED and p.carrier)

    def _models_cb(self, msg):
        """Where every box is. Boxes are named order_<customer>; that prefix is
        a contract with the dispatcher's _Job.box."""
        for name, pose in zip(msg.name, msg.pose):
            if name.startswith('order_'):
                self._box_pose[name] = (pose.position.x, pose.position.y,
                                        pose.position.z)

    def _task_cb(self, msg):
        """A job from the dispatcher.

        Assignments are sticky, so unlike the continuously-retasked siblings of
        this node most of these messages are simply the dispatcher restating
        the job already in hand, and the common case is to ignore them. The
        two that matter are a genuinely new parcel and the dispatcher
        confirming a delivery it has verified on the ground.
        """
        same = self._parcel is not None and msg.id == self._parcel.id

        if same and msg.state == Parcel.DELIVERED:
            self._delivered += 1
            self._history.append(
                (self._delivered, '%s -> %s' % (msg.src_name, msg.dst_name)))
            self._print_delivery(self._delivered, msg)
            self._parcel = None
            self._awaiting = False
            self._descend_since = None
            self._state = 'CLIMB'
            return

        if msg.state not in (Parcel.CLAIMED, Parcel.CARRIED):
            return

        self._parcel = msg

        if self._awaiting:
            # Released, and the dispatcher has come back with work rather than
            # a delivery: the box did not land where it should have, and this
            # is the same parcel re-listed as a fresh pickup. The leg follows
            # from the empty gripper, so there is nothing to reset but the
            # waiting flag and the approach.
            self._awaiting = False
            self._descend_since = None
            print('  [%s] %s[RE-LISTED]%s parcel %d  %s -> %s'
                  % (self._ns, _RED, _RESET, msg.id, msg.src_name, msg.dst_name))
            if self._state not in ('TAKEOFF', 'ACT'):
                self._state = 'ALIGN'
            return

        if same:
            # The dispatcher restating the job already in hand, which is most
            # of these. Taking the message keeps the addresses fresh; the
            # source can move under us if the parcel was re-listed.
            return

        self._descend_since = None
        print('  [%s] %s[JOB]%s parcel %d  %s -> %s'
              % (self._ns, _CYAN, _RESET, msg.id, msg.src_name, msg.dst_name))
        if self._state not in ('TAKEOFF', 'ACT'):
            self._state = 'ALIGN'

    # -------------------------------------------------------- control loop --
    def _control_loop(self, _event):
        if not self._odom_ready:
            return

        cmd = Twist()

        # Lost aircraft first, before any state does anything with it. There
        # is no command that helps here - see "Geofence and rescue" above.
        # Except just after a respawn, when the attitude being read is a drone
        # bouncing on its landing gear rather than one in trouble.
        if rospy.Time.now() < self._settle_until:
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        if self._lost():
            self._rescue()
            return

        # TRAFFIC ---------------------------------------------------------
        # Checked before any phase builds a command, and after the rescue
        # check, so that giving way applies to transiting, descending and
        # climbing alike. TAKEOFF is exempt: an aircraft on the ground is not
        # a hazard to anybody and cannot get out of the way in any case.
        if self._state != 'TAKEOFF':
            other, gap = self._traffic_conflict()
            if other is not None:
                if not self._yielding:
                    rospy.loginfo('[%s] giving way to %s, %.2f m', self._ns,
                                  other, gap)
                    self._yield_began = rospy.Time.now()
                self._yielding = True
                self._cmd_pub.publish(self._give_way(cmd, other))
                return
            if self._yielding:
                # Time spent giving way is not time spent failing to converge,
                # so hand it back to the descent timeout rather than letting
                # good manners cost the aircraft its parcel.
                if self._descend_since is not None and self._yield_began:
                    self._descend_since += rospy.Time.now() - self._yield_began
                self._yielding = False
                self._yield_z = None
                self._yield_began = None

        # TAKEOFF ---------------------------------------------------------
        if self._state == 'TAKEOFF':
            error = self._cruise_alt - self._z
            if abs(error) < 0.15:
                self._state = 'WAIT'
                self._wait_ticks = 2
                rospy.loginfo('[%s] airborne at %.2f m - on task', self._ns, self._z)
            cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * error, -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        if self._parcel is None or (self._awaiting and self._state != 'CLIMB'):
            # Airborne with nothing to fly at: between a delivery and the next
            # assignment, waiting on a verdict, or more parcels claimed than
            # there are aircraft. Hold cruise altitude rather than publishing an
            # empty Twist, which commands zero velocity and lets the drone sink
            # - see the note in DESCEND.
            self._state = 'IDLE'
            cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (self._cruise_alt - self._z),
                                  -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        # CLIMB -----------------------------------------------------------
        # Get back up before flying anywhere. Checked before the geofence and
        # before any horizontal command is built, because the whole point is
        # that nothing else happens until the aircraft has its height back.
        if self._state == 'CLIMB':
            error = self._cruise_alt - self._z
            cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * error, -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
            if abs(error) <= self._climb_tol:
                self._state = 'ALIGN'
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        # Soft geofence. Outside it, the middle of the yard becomes the goal
        # and the parcel waits - a drone drifting toward the wall it is about
        # to be upset by should not still be working a delivery. The parcel is
        # not given back: it is still this drone\'s job, and it may well still
        # be hanging underneath it.
        if self._outside_fence():
            tx, ty = 0.0, 0.0
            self._fenced = True
            rospy.logwarn_throttle(
                5.0, '[%s] outside the fence at (%.1f, %.1f) - returning'
                % (self._ns, self._x, self._y))
        else:
            self._fenced = False
            tx, ty = self._goal()

        dx = tx - self._x
        dy = ty - self._y
        dist_xy = math.hypot(dx, dy)

        # Altitude is driven in every flying state, because it is independent
        # of heading and there is no reason to give up height while turning.
        # Cruise everywhere except DESCEND, which overrides this below - both
        # ends of the job are on the ground, so there is no per-goal altitude
        # to track on the way there.
        cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (self._cruise_alt - self._z),
                              -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)

        # WAIT ------------------------------------------------------------
        if self._state == 'WAIT':
            self._wait_ticks -= 1
            if self._wait_ticks <= 0:
                self._state = 'ALIGN'
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        # DESCEND / ACT ---------------------------------------------------
        # Overhead the goal: hold station over it and come down, then do the
        # thing this leg exists for. Entered from CHASE, and left by the action
        # succeeding, by drifting off the goal, or by the timeout.
        if self._state in ('DESCEND', 'ACT') or (self._state == 'CHASE' and
                                                 not self._fenced and
                                                 dist_xy <= self._arrive_rad):
            # Do not drop into an occupied depot. Hold station over the goal at
            # this aircraft's own transit layer instead, where it is separated
            # from every other by construction, and go down when the depot is
            # free. This is the piece the altitude layers cannot do for
            # themselves: they separate transits perfectly and then all meet at
            # the descent.
            if self._state != 'ACT' and not self._descent_clear():
                self._state = 'HOLD'
                self._descend_since = None
                bx = dx * math.cos(self._yaw) + dy * math.sin(self._yaw)
                by = -dx * math.sin(self._yaw) + dy * math.cos(self._yaw)
                cmd.linear.x = _clamp(self._stn_gain * bx,
                                      -self._stn_speed, self._stn_speed)
                cmd.linear.y = _clamp(self._stn_gain * by,
                                      -self._stn_speed, self._stn_speed)
                rospy.loginfo_throttle(
                    5.0, '[%s] holding at %.1f m - depot busy' %
                    (self._ns, self._cruise_alt))
                self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
                return

            if self._state != 'DESCEND':
                self._state = 'DESCEND'
            if self._descend_since is None:
                self._descend_since = rospy.Time.now()

            if dist_xy > 2.0 * self._arrive_rad:
                # Blown off the goal. Climbing first rather than chasing at
                # working height keeps the transit rules honest - and with a
                # box slung underneath, crossing the yard at 0.31 m is how a
                # parcel gets dragged through a crate.
                self._descend_since = None
                self._state = 'CLIMB'
                self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
                return

            # A descent that will not converge has to end, or the parcel is
            # claimed forever and no other aircraft is ever offered it. This
            # is the only route by which a courier gives a job back.
            if (rospy.Time.now() - self._descend_since).to_sec() > self._act_timeout:
                self._abandon('descent did not converge in %.0f s'
                              % self._act_timeout)
                self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
                return

            centred = dist_xy <= self._grasp_tol
            at_height = abs(self._z - self._work_alt) <= self._grasp_z_tol

            # Two levels, not one. Off-centre the aircraft actively holds a
            # hover altitude above the box while it centres; centred, it comes
            # down the last stretch to the working altitude. Sinking onto a box
            # you are 5 cm off is how you knock it across the yard, and on the
            # delivery leg it is how a box ends up on its corner.
            #
            # The hover leg has to be a commanded *altitude*, not a commanded
            # zero rate, and that distinction cost a whole run to find.
            # controller/twist closes its loop on velocity and nothing in the
            # stack closes one on position, so linear.z = 0 is not "hold this
            # height" - it is "stop climbing", and the aircraft sinks at about
            # a centimetre a second underneath it. Off-centre for twenty
            # seconds is twenty centimetres of altitude quietly given away,
            # which puts the drone below the grasp window it is trying to hit
            # and, since nothing brings it back up while it is still
            # off-centre, out of the running for good. Measured: three drones,
            # 189 seconds in DESCEND between them, no grasps at all.
            target_z = (self._work_alt if centred
                        else self._work_alt + self._hover_margin)
            target_z = max(target_z, self._min_alt)
            cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (target_z - self._z),
                                  -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)

            # Station-keeping, in the body frame. The world-frame error is
            # rotated by yaw because /cmd_vel is body-stabilized; this is the
            # one phase that commands lateral velocity, and translating
            # sideways is exactly what makes the laser sectors meaningless.
            bx = dx * math.cos(self._yaw) + dy * math.sin(self._yaw)
            by = -dx * math.sin(self._yaw) + dy * math.cos(self._yaw)
            cmd.linear.x = _clamp(self._stn_gain * bx,
                                  -self._stn_speed, self._stn_speed)
            cmd.linear.y = _clamp(self._stn_gain * by,
                                  -self._stn_speed, self._stn_speed)

            if centred and at_height:
                self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
                self._act()
                return

            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        target_angle = math.atan2(dy, dx)
        angle_err = _normalize_angle(target_angle - self._yaw)

        # ALIGN -----------------------------------------------------------
        if self._state == 'ALIGN':
            if abs(angle_err) <= self._angle_tol:
                self._state = 'CHASE'
            else:
                cmd.angular.z = _clamp(self._ang_gain * angle_err,
                                       -self._max_ang, self._max_ang)
                self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
                return

        # CHASE -----------------------------------------------------------
        # Drop back to ALIGN if the heading has drifted badly - but not while
        # avoiding, and not for avoid_hold afterwards. The safety filter yaws
        # deliberately off-target, and tb3_treasure's unconditional version of
        # this check would read that as drift, hand control to ALIGN, and turn
        # straight back into the pillar the filter had just steered around.
        #
        # Suppressing it only while the filter is *currently* triggered is not
        # enough, and the reason is that clearing the front sector is the one
        # thing a successful deflection guarantees. The drone turns off the
        # pillar, the sector clears, this check fires on the same tick, ALIGN
        # takes over and yaws it straight back, and the pillar is in front
        # again - now with no forward motion to show for it, because ALIGN does
        # not translate. That is a livelock, not a wobble: a drone 2.5 m from
        # its target ping-ponged ALIGN/CHASE for the last 90 seconds of a run
        # and finished with two collections against another drone's twelve.
        # The hold buys enough committed forward flight - about 0.9 m at cruise
        # for every second of it - to actually pass the thing, which is a
        # slightly longer lateral offset than a tree plus a rotor span.
        if (abs(angle_err) > 2.0 * self._angle_tol and
                rospy.Time.now() >= self._avoid_until):
            self._state = 'ALIGN'
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        speed_factor = (max(0.3, dist_xy / self._slow_dist)
                        if dist_xy < self._slow_dist else 1.0)

        # Body-frame forward. hector rotates this by the current yaw itself -
        # see "Which frame /cmd_vel is in" at the top of this file - so flying
        # along the heading is what commanding x alone already means.
        cmd.linear.x = self._lin_speed * speed_factor
        cmd.linear.y = 0.0
        cmd.angular.z = _clamp(self._ang_gain * angle_err,
                               -self._max_ang, self._max_ang)

        cmd = self._safety_filter(cmd)

        self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)


    # ---------------------------------------------------------- the action --
    def _act(self):
        """Do the thing this leg exists for: grasp, or release.

        Called once the aircraft is both centred and at working height. On the
        pickup leg a successful weld switches the goal to the far address and
        the same four phases run again; on the delivery leg the release is the
        end of this node\'s involvement, and the parcel is not counted until
        the dispatcher has looked at where the box actually came to rest.
        """
        self._state = 'ACT'

        if self._leg == 'PICKUP':
            if self._grasp():
                self._descend_since = None
                self._state = 'CLIMB'
            else:
                self._abandon('could not weld the box on')
            return

        # DELIVERY. Release, then climb off it. The box is left exactly where
        # it was welded, which is why the descent had to be centred: the
        # dispatcher measures the landing against the depot, not against the
        # drone\'s opinion of where it is.
        self._release()
        self._report(Parcel.DELIVERED)
        self._descend_since = None
        # The parcel is deliberately not cleared here, and the aircraft stops
        # working it until the dispatcher rules. The box is down, but whether
        # that counts is measured against the depot rather than against this
        # node\'s opinion - and if it does not count, the job comes straight
        # back as a fresh pickup, quite possibly to this same aircraft.
        #
        # Without the wait the courier would climb, re-align on a goal that is
        # now a *pickup* (because it is no longer holding anything), fly back
        # down onto the box it has just delivered and collect it again.
        self._awaiting = True
        self._state = 'CLIMB'


def main():
    Courier()
    rospy.spin()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
